## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. For primitives (`string`, `number`), change means value change. For objects and functions, React uses **referential equality** (`===`) — two objects with identical contents are still different references if created separately.

```jsx
// This creates a NEW object on every render of Parent
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// This creates a NEW function on every render of Parent
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every time `Parent` renders, `Child` gets new prop references → `Child` re-renders even if the data is semantically identical.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|------|-----------------|----------|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props didn't change | Child is pure and renders often with same props |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recalculates only when deps change | Expensive computation, or stable object identity for props |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — same reference across renders | Passing callbacks to memoized children or effect deps |

**`useMemo` vs `useCallback`:**
```js
useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b])   // returns the value
useCallback(() => doThing(a, b), [a, b])              // returns the function
// useCallback(fn, deps) === useMemo(() => fn, deps)
```

**Don't over-apply them.** Memoization has overhead. Use it when:
- The child component is wrapped in `React.memo`
- The value is used in a `useEffect` dependency array
- The computation is genuinely expensive

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context triggers a re-render in **every consumer** whenever the context value changes reference.

```jsx
// Bad: new object created on every Parent render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

The `{ user, setUser }` object literal is a new reference each time `AuthProvider` renders. Every consumer re-renders.

**Fix:** memoize the value, or split stable and unstable context:

```jsx
// Better: stable reference when user hasn't changed
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Even better for high-frequency updates: split into `UserContext` (changes) and `UserDispatchContext` (stable `setUser`), so action-only consumers never re-render on state changes.

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before — every keystroke re-renders `ExpensiveList`:**

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // New array reference on every render
  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.includes(query));

  // New function reference on every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => console.log('selected', item);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// No memo — always re-renders when parent does
function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `query` changes:**

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // Stable reference — recomputes only when items or query changes
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  // Stable reference — never changes (no deps)
  const handleSelect = useCallback(
    (item) => console.log('selected', item),
    []
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips re-render when props are referentially equal
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* ... */
});
```

**Key takeaway:** `React.memo` is the gate; `useMemo`/`useCallback` are what make props stable enough to pass through it. All three are needed together — `React.memo` alone won't help if props are new references every render.
